Cauchy's theorem gives elements $r$ and $s$ of orders $3$ and $2$. The subgroup generated by $r$ has index $2$, so the other coset is both $s lr(chevron.l r chevron.r)$ and $lr(chevron.l r chevron.r) s$. Thus $s r = r^i s$. The cases $i = 1$ and $i = 2$ give $C_6$ and $D_6$ respectively; $i = 0$ is impossible.
